          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/consistent-hashing.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Consistent Hashing\\ndescription: A technique for distributing cache keys and nodes onto a circular keyspace to minimize remapping when nodes are added or removed.\\ntimestamp: 2026-08-17T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - pattern/ConsistentHashing\\n  - concept/DistributedSystems\\n  - concept/Caching\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source describing the technique; no corroboration yet.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nConsistent hashing is a distributed hashing technique that maps both cache keys and cache nodes onto the same circular keyspace. This approach minimizes the data that needs to be remapped when the cluster topology changes.\\n\\n## How It Works\\n\\nIn a traditional hash table, adding or removing a bucket requires rehashing the entire dataset. Consistent hashing avoids this cost by using a circular structure:\\n\\n- Both cache keys and cache nodes are mapped to positions on a ring.\\n- A key is assigned to the nearest node encountered clockwise on the ring.\\n- When a node is added or removed, only the keys that fall between the new node and its predecessor neighbour require remapping.\\n\\nThis is in contrast to naive hashing schemes, where adding or removing a single node remaps approximately 1/n of all keys (where n is the number of nodes).\\n\\n## Virtual Nodes\\n\\nA small number of physical nodes on a real ring creates load imbalance: some nodes receive far more keys than others due to the uneven distribution of randomly placed nodes. **Virtual nodes** (also called **replica tokens**) solve this by assigning multiple ring positions to each physical node.\\n\\nEach physical node is represented by several virtual nodes on the ring. This spread allows more even distribution of keys across the physical cluster, even when the number of nodes is small. When a physical node is removed, its virtual nodes are removed together, and the load redistributes smoothly across remaining nodes.\\n\\n## Applications\\n\\nConsistent hashing is widely used in:\\n\\n- Distributed caches (e.g. memcached, Redis cluster mode)\\n- Distributed storage systems\\n- Load balancing\\n- Content delivery networks\\n- Peer-to-peer systems\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://log-no-day-grouping/sample-09 \\u2014 Consistent hashing ring definition and virtual nodes explanation.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
